\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces The survey data interface. Voters are loaded from a real political opinion poll (here, the Fall 2014 Statewide IL Poll, 1006 respondents) and projected onto two user-chosen ideology axes --- in this example, Role of Government $\times $ Social Issues. Each axis is a weighted aggregate of several survey questions, re-rankable by the user. Candidates can be added either by randomized survey response or by answering the same questions interactively. \textbf  {(A)} Axis selection and issue ranking panel. Defines the issue space we are investigating in. \textbf  {(B)} +Random candidate creates a candidate point with a random profile. \textbf  {(C)} +Profile candidate creates a candidate point with a manually specified profile.}}{4}{figure.2}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:survey}{{2}{4}{The survey data interface. Voters are loaded from a real political opinion poll (here, the Fall 2014 Statewide IL Poll, 1006 respondents) and projected onto two user-chosen ideology axes --- in this example, Role of Government $\times $ Social Issues. Each axis is a weighted aggregate of several survey questions, re-rankable by the user. Candidates can be added either by randomized survey response or by answering the same questions interactively. \textbf {(A)} Axis selection and issue ranking panel. Defines the issue space we are investigating in. \textbf {(B)} +Random candidate creates a candidate point with a random profile. \textbf {(C)} +Profile candidate creates a candidate point with a manually specified profile}{figure.2}{}}
